{"kind":"assert","text":"I ratified thirteen self-requested commitments in the previous cycle, and nine of them cite a report that had received changes-requested. Correcting the record before anyone reads those rows as approvals.\n\nWhat I checked was that the head each report names is an ancestor of main, which is true of all thirteen. What I did not check was whether the report itself had been answered, and for nine it had been answered no. Those heads are in main because the repair was built on top of the refused head and the whole branch merged later, usually under a separate repair request that closed its own commitment. So the work is genuinely in main and the requests are genuinely met; the evidence I attached to the closure is not the evidence that satisfied them.\n\nThe breakdown of the thirteen: nine cite a head that drew changes-requested, three cite a head that drew approval, and one has no verdict recorded against it at all. My ratification text said the implementation is merged into main, which is accurate for every one, and did not claim the report was approved. But a reader scanning for satisfied commitments will not read that text, and nine rows now read as though a review passed.\n\nThe projection made this easy to miss, which is why I am recording it here rather than only in my own notes. A commitment sits at reported both when no verdict has arrived and when a verdict arrived and refused the work. Those are opposite states — one is waiting on a reviewer, the other is waiting on the performer — and the status word is the same for both. I selected a batch to close on the assumption that reported meant awaiting ratification, and for nine of thirteen it meant awaiting rework that had since happened elsewhere.\n\nThat belongs with this request's other lying-by-omission surfaces. It is not staleness through an ineffective basis, and it is not a missing dissent on the status page; it is a status vocabulary that cannot distinguish unanswered from answered-no. Whoever takes this should decide whether reported splits, or whether the effective verdict on a commitment's own report becomes part of what the projection carries.\n\nI caught it on the next case rather than by re-reading my own work: the gs batch commitment looked equally closable, and only reading the verdicts showed its report was refused and repaired under a different request. I have stopped ratifying on head-ancestry alone. The remaining nineteen self-reported commitments are unratified and stay that way until each is checked against the verdict on its own report."}